      "text": "To find the distance between the foci of the ellipse, we start by noting the given information about the ellipse. The ellipse is tangent to the \\(x\\)-axis at \\((4, 0)\\) and tangent to the \\(y\\)-axis at \\((0, 1)\\). This means that the semi-major axis \\(a\\) is 4 and the semi-minor axis \\(b\\) is 1. The standard form of the equation of an ellipse with axes parallel to the coordinate axes is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2}{a^2} + \\frac{y^2}{b^2} = 1\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\), we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2}{16} + \\frac{y^2}{1} = 1\n\\]\n\nThe distance between the foci of an ellipse is given by \\(2c\\), where \\(c\\) is the distance from the center to each focus. The relationship between \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\) is given by \\(c^2 = a^2 - b^2\\). Substituting the values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\), we get:\n\n\\[\nc^2 = 4^2 - 1^2 = 16 - 1 = 15\n\\]\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\[\nc = \\sqrt{15}\n\\]\n\nThe distance between the foci is:\n\n\\[\n2c = 2\\sqrt{15}\n\\]\n\nThus, the distance between the foci of the ellipse is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\sqrt{15}}\n\\]",
