      "text": "Given that the ellipse is tangent to the $x$-axis at $(4, 0)$ and tangent to the $y$-axis at $(0, 1)$, we can deduce that the center of the ellipse is at the point $(4, 1)$ and the lengths of the semi-major and semi-minor axes are $a = 4$ and $b = 1$, respectively. The standard form of the equation of the ellipse is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(x-h)^2}{a^2} + \\frac{(y-k)^2}{b^2} = 1\n\\]\n\nwhere $(h, k)$ is the center of the ellipse. Substituting $h = 4$ and $k = 1$, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(x-4)^2}{4^2} + \\frac{(y-1)^2}{1^2} = 1\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(x-4)^2}{16} + \\frac{(y-1)^2}{1} = 1\n\\]\n\nThe distance between the foci of the ellipse is given by $2c$, where $c = \\sqrt{a^2 - b^2}$. In this case, $a^2 = 16$ and $b^2 = 1$, so:\n\n\\[\nc = \\sqrt{16 - 1} = \\sqrt{15}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the distance between the foci is:\n\n\\[\n2c = 2\\sqrt{15}\n\\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\sqrt{15}}\n\\]",
